package wasdk:platform@2.2.0;

/// Parallel work, as the web's workers do it: another instance of the program's own code, on a
/// thread of its own, sharing no memory with the program and sending it messages, each a list of
/// bytes, over a stream each way.
///
/// **An entry names what a worker runs.** The program exports `worker-entry`, and starting a
/// worker makes a new instance of the program's code and calls its `run` with the entry and the
/// worker's inbox, the stream of what the program sends it; the worker answers the stream of what
/// it sends back, and does its work after it answered. An entry the program does not have answers
/// `unknown-entry`, once the new instance has said so.
///
/// **A worker has the program's IO side and nothing of its user's side.** It reaches the program's
/// files, network and machines, under the program's grants, its fonts, its clocks, random numbers
/// and the GPU (`wasdk:gpu`); it has no views, windows, menus, clipboard, dialogs, notifications, sound, players
/// or cameras, and is asked nothing of the program's life, each answering as on a platform that has
/// none: `views` lists no view and refuses a request with `unsupported`, an optional getter answers
/// none, and `lifecycle`'s requests to end and to open end at once, since they are the main
/// instance's to hear. A worker may start workers of its own.
///
/// **A worker is part of its program.** All of a program's instances read one clock, are frozen
/// and thawed together, count as the program in what it costs, and end when the program ends.
/// Dropping a `worker` ends it where it stands.
interface workers {
  /// Why a worker did not start.
  variant worker-error {
    /// The program's code has no entry of this name.
    unknown-entry(string),
    /// The platform did not start the worker: the program has as many workers as it may, or the
    /// new instance failed before it answered; why.
    refused(string),
  }

  /// A running instance of this program's own code, sharing nothing with it. Dropping it ends it.
  resource worker {
    /// What the worker sends, once: the stream its `run` answered, in the order it wrote. Ends
    /// when the worker drops its end or ends.
    messages: func() -> option<stream<list<u8>>>;
    /// When it ended, and why if it failed: a trap, or an exit with a code other than 0. A worker
    /// has ended once everything it started has finished, its `run` returned and its outbox
    /// written.
    finished: async func() -> result<_, string>;
  }

  /// The program's workers.
  resource workers {
    /// Starts a worker at `entry`, which reads `inbox`; answers once its `run` has accepted the
    /// entry. What the program writes to `inbox` waits until the worker reads it, as what the
    /// worker writes waits until the program reads its messages.
    start: async func(entry: string, inbox: stream<list<u8>>) -> result<worker, worker-error>;
    /// How many threads the machine runs at once, as Rust's `available_parallelism` and the
    /// web's `hardwareConcurrency` say: how many workers keep it busy.
    available-parallelism: func() -> u32;
  }

  /// The program's workers, where it may have any: none where its code exports no `worker-entry`.
  get-workers: func() -> option<workers>;
}

/// Exported by a program with workers; WaOS calls it in each new instance.
interface worker-entry {
  use workers.{worker-error};

  /// Runs the worker at `entry` on `inbox`, answering the stream of what it sends, or
  /// `unknown-entry` where the program has no such entry. The work goes on after the answer, until
  /// everything the call started has finished.
  run: async func(entry: string, inbox: stream<list<u8>>) -> result<stream<list<u8>>, worker-error>;
}
